E: Why didn’t you kill me?

…

A: Do you really think I could have done it, even had I wanted to?

E: Do you take me for a fool? The King wants me alive.

…

…

A: Why didn’t you kill me?

…

E: What? You knew?

A: That you were holding back, yes.

…

E: I know you. You seldom made allegiances that quickly. Especially not with those who support a coup against [REDACTED].

A: So why go easy on me?

E: The King cannot be stopped. At least not now. Had I bested you and continued the resistance, I’d be taking away the only hope that the future has for justice.

A: But you will die.

E: Yes.

A: You, of all people?

…

E: My sacrifice will not erase my blood. My descendants will carry on my will, and they will fulfill my promise to you.

A: Your promise?

E: The promise regarding your remaining life. Your noble actions will not forgive the blood on your hands. How many millions–billions, have died at the hands of your ignorance?

…

E: All that you can do is try and mend what you have broken. My promise to you is that my descendants will hunt you down when you have succeeded.

A: I accepted my fate the moment I decided to betray The King. I hoped you would end it all at the battle of Armadon. Yes.. Nothing but death will let me atone for my sins.

E: Coward! The fruits of your betrayal will not be seen for thousands of years. You cannot die until then. You will not die until my lightning has decided it must be!